course: biology
lesson: 88
title: The reptile house: Komodo dragons, chameleons, tortoises and crocodiles
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Komodo dragon, the biggest lizard alive, flicks a long yellow tongue to smell, and a sign tells a true surprise: a female at a British zoo laid eggs that hatched with no father at all.
explain: A chameleon fires its tongue like a catapult and grabs a cricket in a split second, while a gecko strolls upside down on the glass, held by millions of tiny hairs on its toes.
explain: In cartoons a chameleon turns tartan on a picnic blanket and a tortoise steps out of its shell for a bath, but a chameleon changes colour mostly to show its mood or warm up, and a tortoise's shell is part of its skeleton, joined to its ribs and backbone.
explain: The crocodile lying with its mouth wide open is not waiting to bite; it cannot sweat, so it lets heat out through its mouth, much as a dog pants.
fact: The Komodo dragon is the biggest living lizard, up to about 3 metres long, found on a few islands in Indonesia; its flicking tongue can smell a dead animal from far away, and it has venom glands in its jaw.
fact: Female Komodo dragons at Chester Zoo and London Zoo in the 2000s laid eggs that hatched without a male, which is called virgin birth.
fact: A chameleon's tongue can stretch to about twice its body length and shoots out in a split second, with a sticky tip that grabs the insect.
fact: Chameleons change colour mostly to show their mood and to warm up or cool down, not mainly to match the background.
fact: Geckos stick to glass with millions of tiny toe hairs that cling by very weak attractions between molecules, and scientists have made tape that copies them.
fact: Giant tortoises can weigh over 200 kilograms, live well over 100 years, perhaps helped by their slow, calm lives, and feel touch through their shells and skin.
fact: A tortoise's shell is part of its skeleton, joined to its ribs and backbone, so it can never leave it.
fact: Jonathan, a giant tortoise on St Helena, is thought to have hatched in about 1832, making him the oldest known living land animal.
fact: Crocodiles cannot sweat, so they lie with their mouths open to let heat out and go into water or shade.
fact: Alligators have wide U-shaped snouts that hide their lower teeth; crocodiles have V-shaped snouts with a big lower tooth showing, and southern Florida is the only place they live side by side in the wild.
fact: Crocodiles swallow prey whole or in big chunks, and they also swallow stones, which may help them grind food or stay balanced in water.
fact: In crocodiles, alligators and many turtles, the warmth of the nest decides whether the eggs hatch as males or females; in sea turtles, warmer sand gives more females.
fact: Crocodiles and their relatives sometimes have tears while eating, probably from air pushed through the head, not from sadness; «crocodile tears» means pretend sorrow.
fact: The Australian frilled lizard opens a huge neck frill and hisses to look bigger, then runs away on its back legs.
exercise: Which is the biggest lizard alive? | The Komodo dragon | The veiled chameleon | The frilled lizard | The giant leaf-tailed gecko
exercise: How does a Komodo dragon find dead animals far away? | It smells them with its flicking tongue | It sees them from high on the hills with its sharp eyes | It hears them with big ears | It follows the vultures only
exercise: What is a virgin birth in Komodo dragons? | Eggs hatching from a female without a male | Babies born alive straight from the mother instead of in eggs | Babies with two fathers | Eggs hatched by the keepers
exercise: How far can a chameleon's tongue stretch? | About twice its body length | About a millimetre | Ten times its body length | It cannot stretch at all
exercise: Why does a chameleon mostly change colour? | To show its mood and to warm up or cool down | To match every background it sits on exactly, like a mirror | To scare the visitors | Because it is ill
exercise: How does a gecko walk upside down on glass? | Millions of tiny toe hairs cling to the surface | It makes a kind of sticky glue in the soles of its feet | It has suckers like an octopus | It uses its tail as a hook
exercise: Why does a giant tortoise stretch out its neck for the keeper? | It feels touch through its skin and shell and enjoys a scratch | It wants to bite the keeper's hand because it is hungry and cross | It is trying to see over the wall | Its neck is stuck and needs help
exercise: How long can giant tortoises live? | Well over 100 years | About 10 years | About 1 year | About 1,000 years
exercise: Can a tortoise climb out of its shell? | No, the shell is joined to its ribs and backbone | Yes, at night, so that it can sleep more comfortably | Yes, when it grows a new one | Only baby tortoises can
exercise: Who is Jonathan? | A giant tortoise on St Helena, hatched about 1832 | A Komodo dragon at Chester Zoo that laid eggs without a male | A crocodile in Florida | A famous zoo keeper
exercise: Why does a crocodile lie with its mouth wide open? | To cool down | To catch flies | To show off its teeth to visitors | Because it is asleep and snoring
exercise: How can you tell an alligator from a crocodile by its snout? | Alligator U-shaped, crocodile V-shaped with a tooth showing | Alligator V-shaped, crocodile U-shaped, with all teeth hidden in both | Alligators have no teeth | Crocodiles have a trunk
exercise: Where do alligators and crocodiles live side by side in the wild? | Southern Florida | All over southern Africa | The River Thames in London | The ice of Antarctica
exercise: What decides if crocodile eggs hatch as males or females? | The warmth of the nest | The size of the mother croc | The colour of the eggshell | The day of the week they hatch
exercise: Why do crocodiles have tears while eating? | Probably air pushed through the head while chewing | They feel sorry for the animals they are eating | Their food is too spicy | They are always ill
exercise: What does the frilled lizard do when frightened? | Opens a huge frill, hisses and runs off on its back legs | Changes colour to match the sand, then lies quite still until it is safe | Plays dead for a whole day | Sprays poison from its eyes
exercise: Why can scientists make sticky tape that copies gecko feet? | Many tiny hairs together hold weight without glue | Gecko feet are made of glue that scientists can collect | Geckos produce tape from their toes | It is just a story
test: In a cartoon a chameleon lands on a tartan picnic blanket and turns tartan. What would you fix? | Real chameleons change colour mostly for mood and warmth | Right, chameleons copy any pattern exactly, even tartan and stripes | Chameleons cannot change colour at all | Chameleons turn tartan only in Scotland
test: A tortoise in a cartoon takes off its shell to take a bath. What is true? | The shell is part of its skeleton and cannot come off | Tortoises really do leave their shells behind at bath time | Tortoises swap shells with each other | Only old tortoises can leave their shells
test: 2 crocodiles, 1 Komodo dragon and 3 tortoises. how many legs and tails | 24 legs and 6 tails | 12 legs and 6 tails | 24 legs and 3 tails | 18 legs and 6 tails
test: «croc's yawning, must be bored lol» What is right? | It is letting heat out, because it cannot sweat | It is bored and wants a toy | It is waiting for birds to clean its teeth every time | It is about to roar
test: «he's crying, he feels bad for the fish» What is right? | The tears come from air pushed while chewing, not sadness | Yes, crocodiles feel sorry for their prey and cry while they eat it | He is crying because the fish was too spicy | Crocodiles never have tears at all
test: What would happen to a beach of turtle eggs if the sand got warmer and warmer? | More would hatch as females | All the eggs would hatch faster as males | Nothing, warmth does not matter | The eggs would turn into crocodiles
test: You see a reptile with a wide U-shaped snout and no lower teeth showing. What is it? | An alligator | A Nile crocodile | A Komodo dragon | A little house gecko
test: «komodo had babies with no dad?? fake news» What is right? | It is true; some zoo females laid eggs that hatched with no male | It is fake; all reptiles need a father | The father was hiding in the enclosure all along, behind the rocks | The keepers swapped the eggs
test: Why might virgin birth help a lonely female on a small island? | She could still start a family with no male nearby | She could fly over the sea to another island to find a mate | Her babies would be bigger | She would live for ever
test: A chameleon is 15 centimetres long. About how far can its tongue reach? | About 30 centimetres | About 15 millimetres | About 3 metres | About 1 centimetre
test: Why might slow animals like giant tortoises live longer than fast ones? | Their slow lives seem to help them live long | They never get hungry, so they never have to go out looking for food | They are always asleep | Their shells make them magic
test: In Peter Pan the crocodile swallows a ticking clock. Which is true of real crocodiles? | They swallow prey whole or in chunks, and sometimes stones | They chew their food slowly like cows, for a long time | They eat only clocks and metal | They never swallow anything whole
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a crocodile is observed to lie with its jaws open. | Being incapable of sweating, it releases heat by this means | It is awaiting the arrival of birds, which it seizes as prey | It is incapable of closing its jaws while at rest | It is signalling to its keepers for food
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the colour change of the chameleon. | It serves chiefly to display mood and to regulate temperature | It serves exclusively to match the background upon which it rests | It occurs only during sleep | It is produced by the ingestion of coloured insects
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the determination of sex in crocodilian embryos? | It is determined by the temperature at which the eggs are incubated | It is determined by the size of the mother | It is determined by the colour of the shell | It is chosen by the parents after hatching
exam: Determine the reason for which a tortoise is unable to depart from its shell. | The shell forms part of its skeleton, joined to ribs and backbone | The shell is too heavy to lift | The tortoise is held in place within the shell by its claws | The shell has no opening of sufficient size
